<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  launchd template for the tartci per-host disk reclaimer.

  Why this agent exists: a tartci host admits work by lease, and the lease has
  a disk axis. When the data volume fills, EVERY lease is denied
  `disk_capacity_exceeded` and the host stops serving entirely. It is not a
  slow host at that point, it is a dead one, and its share of the load moves
  elsewhere without anyone being told. Build directories are what fills it:
  large, regenerable, and until this agent nothing removed them. m5 reached
  14 GiB free on a 3.6 TiB volume with 488 build dirs totalling 1.32 TB, and
  refused 276 leases before anyone noticed.

  Deletion is conservative in the same shape as the reap agent: a directory
  goes only when its name is a build-directory name AND it carries a generated
  -tree marker AND it carries no source marker AND no live build process
  references it AND nothing inside it changed inside the age gate.

  Install shape:

    mkdir -p "$HOME/Library/Logs/tartci"
    python3 scripts/render_launchd_template.py \
      launchd/com.danielraffel.tartci.reclaim.plist.template \
      --set "HOME=$HOME" \
      > "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/com.danielraffel.tartci.reclaim.plist"

    launchctl bootstrap "gui/$(id -u)" "$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents/com.danielraffel.tartci.reclaim.plist"
    launchctl kickstart -k "gui/$(id -u)/com.danielraffel.tartci.reclaim"

  Scan roots are DISCOVERED, not declared. This template deliberately does not
  set TARTCI_RECLAIM_ROOTS: the janitor keeps whichever of ~/Code and
  /Volumes/Workshop/Code the host actually has. A hand-tuned root is the one
  failure the guards cannot catch, because a root that EXISTS but sits on the
  wrong volume passes every check and reports a clean exit 0 forever while the
  volume it was installed to protect fills up. m3 is exactly that shape: it
  keeps code and worktrees on Workshop and still has a populated ~/Code on the
  boot disk. Free space is measured on every volume the roots span, and the
  TARTCI_RECLAIM_FAIL_BELOW_GB floor is judged against the tightest of them, so
  a healthy disk cannot certify a full one. Pressure is scoped the other way,
  per volume: only build directories that live on a volume under
  TARTCI_RECLAIM_PRESSURE_FREE_GB get the shorter
  TARTCI_RECLAIM_PRESSURE_MIN_AGE_DAYS gate. Failing more is the safe
  direction; deleting more is not, and a boot disk dipping below the threshold
  is not a reason to reclaim week-old builds off a volume with terabytes free.

  Depth 5, not 3. Depth 3 reaches Code/<repo>/build and
  Code/agent-worktrees/<worktree>/build-cov, but it cannot see the nest that
  now holds the largest single reclaimable tree on the fleet,
  <root>/<repo>/.claude/worktrees/<worktree>/build, which sits five levels
  below the scan root on every host. On m3 that blind spot was 14.86 GiB of
  the 1020.17 GiB reclaimable under Workshop, 14.64 GiB of it in one
  directory. Depth is the right knob rather than a per-host list of nests:
  one number names the same pattern on m3, m5 and m1, and a nest list rots
  the next time a worktree root moves.

  A deeper walk does not weaken the guards, because the marker, source-marker,
  live-process and age tests are all depth independent. Measured on m3's
  Workshop root, depth 5 exposes 94 build-named directories that depth 3 never
  saw and 88 of them are refused for carrying no generated-tree marker, which
  is exactly what external/skia-build/build, cargo target/debug/build and
  node_modules/*/build are. The 6 that pass are regenerable CMake trees.

  Stop at 5. Deeper scans find no further reclaimable bytes and only cost
  walk time, so 5 is the smallest depth that misses nothing.

  TARTCI_RECLAIM_FAIL_BELOW_GB is the escalation half: a host still under the
  floor after a pass exits non-zero, so launchd records a failing janitor
  instead of the fleet only seeing refused leases.

  TARTCI_RECLAIM_LOG bounds the janitor's own receipt. StandardOutPath below
  is appended to on every pass and nothing else ever truncates it, so without
  this the log grows without limit on the very volume the janitor exists to
  protect. It must name the SAME path as StandardOutPath: disk_reclaim renames
  that file aside at startup once it reaches TARTCI_RECLAIM_LOG_MAX_BYTES and
  keeps TARTCI_RECLAIM_LOG_GENERATIONS of it, so the worst case on disk is
  generations x (max bytes + one pass's output).

  Rename rather than truncate, because launchd opens StandardOutPath fresh on
  every spawn of a StartInterval job. The one-pass overshoot in that bound is
  the direct consequence: the fd this pass inherited still points at the inode
  it just renamed, so the pass that triggers a rotation writes into generation
  1 and the new file starts collecting at the next spawn.

  Hourly, not every 5 minutes like the reap agent: a pass walks the scan roots
  and sizes candidates, and disk fills over days rather than seconds.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.danielraffel.tartci.reclaim</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>$HOME/.local/bin/tartci</string>
        <string>reclaim</string>
        <string>--json</string>
        <string>--fix</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>$HOME</string>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>3600</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>$HOME/Library/Logs/tartci/tartci-reclaim.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>$HOME/Library/Logs/tartci/tartci-reclaim.log</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>$HOME</string>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:$HOME/.local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_MAXDEPTH</key>
        <string>5</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_MIN_AGE_DAYS</key>
        <string>30</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_PRESSURE_FREE_GB</key>
        <string>200</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_PRESSURE_MIN_AGE_DAYS</key>
        <string>7</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_FAIL_BELOW_GB</key>
        <string>60</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_LOG</key>
        <string>$HOME/Library/Logs/tartci/tartci-reclaim.log</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_LOG_MAX_BYTES</key>
        <string>8388608</string>
        <key>TARTCI_RECLAIM_LOG_GENERATIONS</key>
        <string>5</string>
    </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
